# Editorial review — Movement 002, chapters 8–14

## Review status and coverage

This is a fresh-context simulated editorial read by the review seat, with canon and formula evidence supplied. It is not the author's self-review, not a blind cold read, and not human audience testing.

Coverage is the complete supplied movement: seven chapters, chapter 8 through chapter 14, 36,806 prose words. I read them in order. The movement is complete on its own stated range, days 51–69 past the Line.

**Verdict: pass for continuity, story, Reader Standard, protected wording, and source-distance. Formula alignment is mixed rather than failed:** the three primary working-range measures pass, while reading ease and sentence-width remain materially lighter/narrower than the selected targets. No continuity repair is required.

## Unscored reader response

Interest catches immediately at `chapter-08.md:3`, when the mule's pace wakes Cael before he understands why, and tightens when Pike strikes Teague's hold in public. The strongest sustained sequence is the ford: the noon reconnaissance makes every later choice legible, the loft turns observations into a dangerous hypothesis, and the fight pays off the terrain, the one-declaration rule, each companion's practiced refusal, and the stillhounds' capacity to learn. The action has genuine consequence without becoming cruel spectacle.

Interest slips only a little after the ford, during the long recovery-and-accounting stretch in chapters 12–13. That material is worthwhile—the injuries change how the group works, and each companion gets a distinct question—but a thirteen-year-old reader may feel the forward plot pause before the well. The well restores immediate physical pressure and gives Cael an excellent, quiet development beat.

Lira matters most to me in this movement. Her discovery that she is fast without Wind is exhilarating; her later question—`chapter-13.md:185`, “So what am I becoming, exactly, if nobody's measuring?”—turns the injury and the board's silence into a personal loss that Cael cannot solve for her. Brom, Karis, and Seln each receive a comparably specific turn rather than functioning as helpers.

I expect the blank tack and the coming hold slip at `chapter-14.md:455–463` to pull the crew into the next road problem while the quiet-ground phrase waits in Karis's box. Yes, I would continue.

## Reader lenses

Scores are editorial judgments, not audience measurements. “13” is the fluent thirteen-year-old lens; “Adult” is the adult progression-fantasy lens. Confidence is lower for the simulated age lens because no real reader was tested.

| Dimension | 13 | Adult | Exact passage location and reason | Confidence (13 / Adult) |
|---|---:|---:|---|---|
| Opening pull | 9 | 9 | `chapter-08.md:3–35`, from “A mule came over the saddle” through Pike coming out without his keeper's coat. Urgency is physical before it is explained, and the boy, exhausted mule, and silent meadow make the cost human. | Medium / High |
| Keep reading | 8 | 9 | The chapter-10 close (`chapter-10.md:289–299`, Lira pointing “Behind you”) and chapter-11 opening convert one cliff into a new tactical problem. The only lull is the extended recovery/accounting run before the well; the adult lens is more likely to value all of its thematic accumulation. | Medium / High |
| Interest / freshness | 9 | 9 | `chapter-09.md:133–205` turns animal behavior, water, discharge, and a tracker’s “cut” into a novel plan; `chapter-14.md:177–221` makes bad air and a thorn faggot a second, wholly different progression problem. | Medium / High |
| Clarity / flow | 8 | 9 | `chapter-08.md:217–283` establishes the sill, lip, pools, bar, Brom's stone, and Lira's run before the fight. The geography pays off cleanly, though a younger reader must hold near/far banks, head/tail, and three groups of hounds in mind through `chapter-10.md:69–155`. | Medium / High |
| Character attachment | 9 | 9 | `chapter-13.md:177–235` lets Lira state the actual loss and lets Cael understand why both reassurance and a replacement score would be wrong. Comparable attachment beats land for Karis at `chapter-11.md:31–55`, Brom at `chapter-13.md:291–341`, and Seln at `chapter-13.md:247–275`. | Medium / High |
| Humor / warmth | 8 | 9 | Humor stays character-specific: Seln's cat-and-registrar comparison (`chapter-09.md:145–151`), Karis versus Lira's semicolon (`chapter-12.md:151–171`), and Brom's “He's only wrong” (`chapter-14.md:283–293`). It relieves rather than cancels pain. | Medium / High |
| Action / suspense | 9 | 9 | `chapter-10.md:69` (“The river had stood up on three sides of them”) through `chapter-11.md:161` develops a changing contest: bait, failed assumptions, the pack testing for a declaration, Brom dropping Skin, Lira refusing Wind, and the pack learning. Costs and positions remain consequential. | Medium / High |
| Progression payoff | 9 | 9 | The hold ceases to be passive restraint and becomes the winning method. `chapter-10.md:119` identifies that the hound is “biting to be answered”; `chapter-10.md:243–271` proves Lira's unassisted speed; `chapter-11.md:167–249` makes Cael price and spend one public Wind burst. Capability changes behavior rather than merely raising a number. | Medium / High |
| Connection | 9 | 9 | The movement cashes Movement 1's buckle, 45-yard spacing, wet gravel, 110-yard question, board procedure, wall position, carter relationship, and practiced hold. It then carries every injury, coin, ledger line, and disclosure through day 69. The final blank tack opens the next pressure without erasing the completed ford and well payoffs. | Medium / High |
| Read-aloud quality | 9 | 9 | Voices are distinguishable without tags: the steading woman's “It's because you die” (`chapter-09.md:109`), Pike's ledger cadence (`chapter-14.md:151`), and Karis's exact dictation. Referents and speaker changes are generally clear; the rehearsed ford geography supports audio comprehension. | Medium / High |

## Continuity, canon, and story audit

No verified contradiction was found.

- **Calendar and joins:** Chapter 8 opens on day 51; the ford is reconnoitred on day 52 and fought on day 53; Thornwater recovery occupies days 54–58; Lowmarch returns on day 60; the well and recoveries carry through day 69. This matches `STATE_LEDGER.md` and the movement brief. Chapter 14's incoming hold slip supplies an effective forward join.
- **Entry state:** All five enter uninjured. They have no cart or horse; the boxes ride the Thornwater carter's load, Seln's closed case is on its strap and later left closed in the loft, and no reserve money is used. The meadow, plank, wall, Pike's double reading, Thornwater relationships, carter's map, weapons, and contextual road hold all inherit Movement 1 correctly.
- **Power limits and deployments:** Cael uses exactly one fragment after the Line: one visible Wind burst at the ford. Its fixed one-count landing lock costs him the calf bite. He considers but refuses his sealed Shadow-adjacent fragment; “Deployment since the Line: one” remains correct through day 69. Seln's underwater use is his own native Shadow Path, not Cael deploying the sealed fragment. No other fragment is used privately or publicly.
- **Stillhound mechanics:** The movement earns the discharge finding rather than assuming it. The water masking, dry-foot preference, first-mover following, cut-dog role, probing bites, and learned refusal to test silent bodies are planted, tested, and then stated as a finding. The two swept downstream remain unconfirmed/open; the prose counts only the five bodies witnessed.
- **Physical state:** Brom's left forearm is opened wrist-to-mid-arm, stitched, reopened at the wrist after he catches the cart, and closed but not trusted by day 66. Lira's right ankle is bound, never mended, and remains late/limited when she runs. Karis's palms prevent writing until day 68. Cael's calf progresses from eleven strides to ordinary level-ground use while still failing uphill. Seln remains uninjured. Nothing heals silently.
- **Money and board arithmetic:** The crew spends one mark before leaving, receives the ford's forty, pays one mark for care, and has 48 in hand; the well raises this to 52. Earned totals move 18 → 58 → 62. The ford becomes the fifth book line and moves the crew from thirteenth to twelfth; the well is the sixth line without changing wall position. Teague is eleven places up.
- **Knowledge boundaries:** Quiet ground remains only the carter's wife's three sentences. Karis deliberately does not ask a fourth question; nobody consults Pike. No healer or Tide practitioner appears. Teague remains absent and does not meet the crew. The mechanism and Cael's integration remain confined to the five.
- **Cause and effect:** The movement's best structural work is that every decisive action grows from prior observation. Brom's stone, Lira's run, Seln's pool, Karis's one point, Cael's public burst, and the pack's first-mover behavior are all established before use. The recovery chapters then translate wounds into altered labor and altered self-concepts rather than treating injury as decorative cost.
- **Plan adherence versus canon:** The mundane bad-air well is author-owned expansion under the packet, not old canon. It fits the stated days, price, and body states and creates no conflict.

## Protected wording and source distance

