   193	The valley's water was fifty yards wide there, where everywhere else on that road it was twenty, and the reason lay under it. A single ledge of grey rock crossed the bed from bank to bank, ten feet broad and as level as a doorstep, and the river slid over it no deeper than a man's shin, fast and clear, with a skin on it like glass. Two grooves had been worn along the ledge by more wheels than he could guess at, and the rock in them and round them was green.
   194	
   195	Above the ledge the river was a long slow glide. Below it there was a row of white stones, each the size of a loaf, set along the rock's downstream edge at intervals of a yard; and beyond the white stones the water was a different colour.
   196	
   197	"That's the lip," said the carter. "Keep your wheel between the grooves and the white, and you're on the rock. Past the white there's nothing." He pointed with his chin again. "You'll see where he'd got to."
   198	
   199	Cael saw. The white stones ran out from the near bank in their row, a dozen of them, and stopped; and for the last five yards before the middle of the river there were none. One lay on the rock by itself, a little out of the line, where somebody had set it down meaning to come back to it.
   200	
   201	In the middle the river had built itself an island. It was a bar of gravel, a long narrow heap of stones that sat on the ledge like a boat run aground upon a weir, with its blunt bow upstream and its stern trailing away below, between the two dark places, to a point. The road came up out of the water onto the bar and crossed it and went down into the water again on the other side.
   202	
   203	"Sill," said the carter, of the ledge, and then, of the green, "Slime. You'll want to walk it flat-footed."
   204	
   205	He took his cart over without another word, and it was the first time Cael had watched him do it as a thing that could go wrong. He led the mule a step at a time. Brom went at the tail-board with both hands on it, and halfway across the near channel the off wheel slid a hand's breadth toward the white stones, quite gently, as a plate slides on a wet table. Brom leaned, and the carter clicked his tongue, and it came back into its groove.
   206	
   207	On the far beach the carter stopped to let the mule blow, forty yards below the crossing, where she could drink without fouling it.
   208	
   209	"I go at my hour," he said. "I've done that for twenty-two years and I'll not stop for forty marks of somebody else's. I'll have your boxes in her loft before the sun's over." He looked at the bar, and then at the five of them, and Cael thought that he had something more to say. But all he did was nod, once, at Brom, as a man nods who has lent a tool.
   210	
   211	Karis stood on the shingle and watched the cart go up the road until the scrub took it. Three boxes went with it, on forty sacks of barley, for the third time.
   212	
   213	"I timed myself," she said. "From when the canvas went out of sight. I looked at the river again in less than a minute." She took a cord out of her coat, with knots in it. "I believe that I'm improving. Will somebody pace for me? I want it in yards, and mine aren't."
   214	
   215	Cael paced it, at a yard to the stride, and she wrote what he called. The near channel was twenty yards from the beach to the gravel, and the far one was twenty. The bar was eight yards across where the road went over it. From its blunt head, a little above the ledge, to the point of its tail, it was thirty.
   216	
   217	They walked every foot of the place, and it took them most of the morning.
   218	
   219	The rock was worse than the carter had said. The green on it was a short close weed, and the water had combed it all one way, and a boot set down on it had no more hold than on a fish. Brom went along the lip on the near side with the bill reversed in his hand, feeling ahead of him with the ash haft as a blind man feels with a stick. Where the white stones stopped he reached out past the edge and let the haft go down. It went in to his fist, and his arm after it to the shoulder, and he drew it up again with nothing on the end.
   220	
   221	"It doesn't shelve," he said. "It stops. There's a wall of rock under that lip, straight down, and I can't find the floor."
   222	
   223	Karis dropped a pebble where he had tried, and watched it sink through the green water, turning, until it was a pale speck and then nothing.
   224	
   225	"Over my head," she said. "And yours. I shan't put a number on it. The pool on the other side of the bar is the same, for I've looked, and they join below the tail." She wrote. "So the lower twenty yards of this gravel have deep water down both flanks and at the end. A person standing at the tip can leave it by one road, which is back up the bar."
   226	
   227	"Unless he swims," said Lira.
   228	
   229	"Unless he swims," Karis agreed. "I wonder whether they do."
   230	
   231	Cael stood on the bar's head and made his own catalogue, as he would have made it of a floor before a bout. There was the near beach, and the cut bank behind it, as high as a man, with grass along its top. There was the far beach, wider, where the mule had stood. He counted the large stones along the far waterline, the ones too big to shift in a flood, since he wished to know that shore when he saw it again in a worse light: seventeen.
   232	
   233	Nothing remained of the forty-ninth evening except an iron bar, lying on the gravel where it had been put down, and the one white stone out of its row. At the tail of the bar the stones had been raked up in long scars, as the sand of a ring is after a bout, and something had since gone over them and smoothed them partly down. It might have been the river.
   234	
   235	Seln was on one knee beside those scars, with his coat tucked up out of the wet, reading.
   236	
   237	---
   238	
   239	Brom tried every stone on that ledge, and he was an hour about it.
   240	
   241	There were a good many. The rock had cracked in old floods, and slabs of it stood up here and there a hand's breadth clear of the water, some no bigger than a plate and some as broad as a table. He went from one to the next like a man buying a horse. He stood on each, and shifted his weight, and came down off it shaking his head.
   242	
   243	"What does he want?" said Lira. She was sitting on the gravel with her boots off, wringing out a stocking.
   244	
   245	"I think he wants to be asked."
   246	
   247	"Brom. What do you want?"
   248	
   249	"A doorstep," said Brom, across twenty feet of water.
