   251	He found it in the near channel, five yards out from the gravel. It was a slab as long as his arm each way, and its top was dry; but the thing that stopped him was its downstream edge. The rock had split there and tilted, long ago, so that a rim stood up along that side to the height of his ankle, like the lip of a dish. He set his left heel against it and looked at them over his shoulder with an expression that Cael had not seen on him since a ferryman's price was agreed.
   252	
   253	"Push me," he said.
   254	
   255	Cael pushed him. He put both hands in the middle of that broad back and leaned with all his weight, and his own boots went out from under him on the green, and Brom did not move by the width of a finger. Lira came out barefoot and tried from the side, and then the two of them tried together.
   256	
   257	"It's the lip," said Brom. "The river wants me off this rock and into that hole, and so will anything that hits me, and my heel says no. I don't have to do a thing but stand." He patted the slab with his boot. "Everything that comes along this ledge from that bank has to come by here. I've a stone."
