   262	
   263	Cael watched her come down it and thought of a painted floor at Norhold. She had been quicker there, with the burst, and she had never looked like this, for there she had been running at a mark. Here she ran thirty yards of loose stones for no one, and stopped because she had decided to.
   264	
   265	"It rolls," she said, walking back up with her hands on her hips. "The spine's firm. You can run on the spine as if it were a road. But the last five yards are round stones lying on round stones, with the water through them, and the edges are worse. If I had to stop there in a hurry I'd want to do it short."
   266	
   267	"Then stop short."
   268	
   269	"I'm telling you so that I'll remember I said it."
   270	
