   282	
   283	Lira was asleep within four breaths. Brom lay with his hands behind his head and his lips moving, and Cael was fairly sure that he was going over the virtues of a rock. Karis wrote for the whole of the two hours, with Hesk's clock propped against her knee, and never once looked up the road after a cart.
   284	
   285	Seln came back when the shadows had only begun to lean.
   286	
   287	He was dry. There was grass seed on his shoulders and down one sleeve, and he sat among them and held out his hand for the water-skin. He drank a good deal of it before he spoke.
   288	
   289	"Seven," he said.
   290	
   291	They waited.
   292	
   293	"Is that all you're going to say?" said Lira.
   294	
   295	"It is all that will keep until there is a roof. The rest wants a floor to draw on, and I should like to draw it once." He handed back the skin and stood. "We have a mile of grass to walk at an hour that no carter would choose. I suggest that we begin."
   296	
   297	"Seven what?" said Karis. She had her pencil ready, and her voice was not altogether steady.
   298	
   299	Seln looked down at her. It seemed to Cael that he was deciding how much a person was owed who had waited a fortnight with one leaf.
   300	
   301	"Seven that came down to drink," he said. "One at a time. And not one of the seven would wet a foot to do it."

