   138	
   139	The hound hit him at the thigh and went for the belly and he took it on the shoulder instead, dropping into it, and its hind feet went out from under it on the slimed stone because there was nothing under them a foot could set, and the current took the hind end and turned the animal broadside, and Brom, planted on a stone he had found on purpose with a lip against its upstream side, put his shoulder down and through it and drove it under. It went with the current and did not come back.
   140	
