   148	
   149	She ran as she ran on a floor in the last minute before a bout, everything going and nothing held, and for the first time since she was fourteen there was no burst at the end of it. Cael saw her want it and saw her go through the beat with nothing in it and keep going, because she was, it turned out, fast. Fast without it. The first hound came at her low and she was not where it arrived; she had gone left on the gravel and it went past her hip and she put the long knife into the back of its neck and out again and it went down on the bar and did not move.
